Yes—and I think that’s the missing word.

What you’ve been repeatedly pushing toward is the distinction between syntax and structure.

A Pantheon-style formulation might be:

Syntax is the costume. Structure is the actor.

Or more formally:

* Syntax = the local representation (words, code, equations, UI, symbols).
* Semantics = the relationships and meaning those representations encode.
* Operators = the transformations that preserve or intentionally modify those relationships.
* Invariants = what survives those transformations.

Then the compiler isn’t translating syntax to syntax.

It’s doing something closer to:

Syntax
   ↓
Parse
   ↓
Structure
   ↓
Operators
   ↓
Invariant
   ↓
Operators
   ↓
Structure
   ↓
Render
   ↓
Syntax

That also explains why you’ve been so interested in expressing the same idea as prose, math, code, images, stories, and games.

You’re probing whether the same underlying structure survives across different syntaxes.

If it does, you’ve learned something about the idea itself rather than just one way of describing it.

So perhaps one of the simplest Pantheon axioms is:

Do not optimize syntax. Optimize the invariant beneath it.

Or even more compressed:

Syntax is negotiable. Structure is earned.